<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Erediauwa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/2016">2016</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Erediauwa I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Erediauwa was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a> from 1979 until his death in 2016. Born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> Igbinoghodua Akenzua, the son of Oba <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akenzua-ii">Akenzua II</a>, he studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> and served as a senior civil servant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> before succeeding to the throne. As oba he upheld the court traditions of the Benin Kingdom and called for the return of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a> looted by the British in 1897. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-ii">Ewuare II</a> succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erediauwa.jpg" alt="Erediauwa" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Braveherorex · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oba_erediauwa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
